        "thinking": "Excellent! Now I have a comprehensive audit. Let me now think about which of these to implement. The user is asking for \"all that would work normally\" in CF mode. Let me focus on the most critical ones that the UI actually uses:\n\n**Priority 1 — UI pages that are actively broken:**\n- GET /api/companies/:companyId/members → used by Access page\n- GET /api/companies/:companyId/invites → used by Invites page  \n- POST/PATCH/DELETE member operations\n\n\n- **Priority 2 — Auth/access flows:**\n- CLI auth routes → used by CLI authentication\n- Invite routes → used by invitation flow\n\n**Priority 3 — Skills management:**\n- GET /api/companies/:companyId/skills/:skillId → skill detail\n- Other skill routes\n\n**Priority 4 — Admin routes:**\n- GET /api/admin/users etc.\n\n**Priority 5 — Plugin routes (low priority since plugins aren't available in CF):**\n- GET /api/plugins/:pluginId etc.\n\nThe most efficient approach would be to make the route files themselves work in CF by wrapping them properly. Actually, wait—let me reconsider. The audit shows all the access.ts routes are pure functions, so I should be able to adapt them directly.\n\nThe real issue is that these routes rely on `assertCompanyPermission` and `assertCompanyAccess`, which are Express middleware-based auth checks that depend on `req.actor` being set by CF's `resolveActorFromRequest`. In `local_trusted` mode, requests are pre-authenticated, so those checks should pass. The question is whether I can just register `accessRoutes` and `companySkillRoutes` in `route-registry.ts` directly—they weren't added before because they use raw Express handlers instead of `expressHandler`-wrapped ones. I need to understand how `extractRoutesFromRouter` handles raw Express handlers and whether it looks for specific handler tags.\n\nRaw handlers without `__handler` tags will be skipped by the route extraction. But there's an alternative: I could use the Express router directly in the CF worker since `cf-express-shim.ts` already bridges between Hono and Express. The existing infrastructure wraps transport-agnostic handlers with `expressHandler`, which tags them so `extractRoutesFromRouter` can find them.\n\nSince `access.ts` routes are pure Express without that wrapper, I need a different approach. I could create a Hono middleware that delegates requests to an Express router using the CF Express shim for request and response objects.",
